(window.COURSE_SOURCES = window.COURSE_SOURCES || {})["grid-down-food"] = "## Sources note\n\nWhich sources were read, and how far. Written 11/10/2026.\n\n**Read in full (page body)**\n- WHO, Five keys to safer food (poster, 2001).\n- UK Food Standards Agency, Food safety in a power cut: advice for consumers (GOV.UK).\n- UK Chief Medical Officers, Food and nutrition scripts for a national power outage (GOV.UK, 16/12/2025).\n- USDA FSIS, Keeping food safe during an emergency, including both save-or-discard tables.\n- CDC, How to make water safe in an emergency.\n- CDC, Home-canned foods and botulism.\n- National Center for Home Food Preservation, For safety's sake (extract of the USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning).\n- Food Safety Authority of Ireland, Storing food safely.\n\n**Read from a long search extract (looked near complete, not opened as a full page)**\n- CDC, Keep food safe after a disaster or emergency.\n- CDC, Drinking water advisories: an overview.\n- UK Health Security Agency, Carbon monoxide: general information.\n\n**Not read**\n- CDC pages on food and water in a radiation emergency (search link only). The course does not teach a radiological module; it only states that boiling does not remove radioactive material, per the CDC water page.\n- The USDA Complete Guide to Home Canning itself (the course deliberately does not teach canning).\n- No specific video was verified.\n\n**Where sources differ (shown side by side in the lessons)**\n- Fridge limit: UK under 8 C in a power cut (FSA), Ireland 0 to 5 C (FSAI), US 40 F (FSIS).\n- Milk, yoghurt and eggs after the fridge fails: FSIS discards perishables above 40 F for over 2 hours; the UK CMO script for a long national outage allows some for days if they look and smell normal. The course says to use the stricter rule when other safe food exists, and always for at-risk people. That recommendation is our reading.\n- Refreezing: FSAI says do not refreeze defrosted food unless first cooked above 75 C; FSIS and CDC allow refreezing while ice crystals remain.\n\n**Our inference or framing (labelled in the lessons)**\n- Scaling arithmetic (for example bleach per litre from the CDC per-gallon ratio), the \"stricter rule\" advice, practical tips such as filling bottles before a warning, and the 72-hour skeleton table.\n\n**Illustrative numbers**\nAll households, dates and times used in examples are invented.\n";
